## 📌 Executive Summary

This notebook investigates the longitudinal evolution of microenvironmental pH during a 21-day simulated body fluid (SBF, pH 7.4 at 37 °C) immersion challenge across phase-programmed Apatite–Wollastonite Glass-Ceramics (AWGCs) sintered from **700 °C to 1100 °C**.

Within the **Kinetic Blueprint** framework, local solution pH provides the critical biophysical bridge between atomic-scale network speciation (NBO/BO ratio), mass-transport kinetics, and downstream cellular viability (*Biomaterials Science*, 2026).

---

## 🔬 Scientific Motivation & Chemical Mechanism

Previous analyses demonstrated that:
- $\uparrow \text{NBO/BO Ratio} \longrightarrow \uparrow \text{Cumulative Mass Loss (\%)}$
- $\uparrow \text{NBO/BO Ratio} \longrightarrow \uparrow \text{Calcium (\text{Ca}^{2+}) and Silicon (\text{Si}^{4+}) Ion Release}$
- $\uparrow \text{Bulk Density (g cm}^{-3}\text{)} \longrightarrow \downarrow \text{Mass Transport / Dissolution Rate } (k)$

### The Interfacial Reaction Mechanism
Glass-ceramic dissolution begins with an **ion-exchange reaction** between hydronium protons ($\text{H}^+$) in SBF and network-modifying cations ($\text{Ca}^{2+}, \text{Mg}^{2+}$) located at Non-Bridging Oxygen ($\text{NBO}$) sites:

$$\equiv \text{Si–O}^-\text{Ca}^{2+}\text{–O}^-\text{Si}\equiv \;\; + \;\; 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \;\; \longrightarrow \;\; 2(\equiv \text{Si–O–H}) \;\; + \;\; \text{Ca}^{2+} \;\; + \;\; \mathbf{2\text{OH}^-}$$

Every cation leached from an NBO site consumes a proton and generates **hydroxyl ions ($\text{OH}^-$)**, directly driving local solution alkalization. Solution pH therefore serves as an immediate, real-time proxy for atomic-scale network depolymerization.

